.TH CEVENTLOOP_DECLARE_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ceventloop_declare_scoped \- declare a ceventloop with automatic destruction on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ceventloop_declare_scoped(name);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a ceventloop variable set to CEVENTLOOP_NULL and
tags it with a cleanup attribute, so the loop is destroyed
automatically, exactly as ceventloop_destroy() would do it, when
the variable leaves its scope. You must assign the variable with
ceventloop_construct() or a ceventloop_create*() call before
you use it.

When the declaration and the initialisation can happen in one place,
which is the common case, use ceventloop_construct_scoped()
instead.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  Variable name to declare.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro uses __attribute__((cleanup)), so it needs GCC or Clang.
Leaving the variable at CEVENTLOOP_NULL for the whole scope is
safe, because the cleanup does nothing for that value.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    ceventloop_declare_scoped(loop);
    char *err = NULL;
    loop = ceventloop_create(32, 1, 1, &err);
    if (!loop) { fprintf(stderr, "%s\en", err); return; }
    /* ... */
}  /* the library destroys loop automatically here */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ceventloop_declare (3),
.BR ceventloop_construct_scoped (3),
.BR ceventloop_create (3),
.BR ceventloop_destroy (3)
